# LOOK(1)

## NAME

look - find lines in a sorted list

## SYNOPSIS

| **look** [ **-df** ] [ **-t**\&*c* ] string [ file ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Look* consults a sorted *file* and prints all lines that begin with *string*. It uses binary
search.

The options **d** and **f** affect comparisons as in sort(1):

**d**
: `Dictionary` order: only letters, digits, tabs and blanks participate in comparisons.

**f**
: Fold. Upper case letters compare equal to lower case.

**-t**\&*c*
: Set the termination character to *c*: the comparison stops there rather than at the end of the
  line.

If no *file* is specified, */usr/dict/words* is assumed with collating sequence **-df**.

## SORTED MEANS SORTED IN BYTE ORDER

*Note:* lines are compared a byte at a time, and a byte is an unsigned value from 0 to 255 on
this machine where it was a signed one on the PDP-11. So a character above U+007F - which is
more than one byte in this system's UTF-8 text - sorts *after* every ASCII character. That is
the order sort(1) produces and the order */usr/dict/words* below is in; **LC_ALL=C sort -c** is
the check. A list sorted any other way will make the binary search miss entries that are there.

The two comparison options are defined over a multi-byte character as follows, which v7 had no
occasion to say: a byte above 0177 is part of a letter, so **-d** keeps it, and a multi-byte
letter has no case here, so **-f** leaves it alone. **look го** therefore finds **год**,
**голова** and **город**.

## FILES

/usr/dict/words

## BUGS

*Note: /usr/dict/words* on this machine is a short list written for the purpose - about a
thousand entries - and not v7's 25,000-word dictionary, which is not in this source tree.
*Look* is a working command over it, but it is not a spelling authority.

A dictionary line longer than 249 bytes is cut before it is compared. *Note:* v7 had no bound
here at all and wrote past the end of its buffer.

## SEE ALSO

sort(1), grep(1), comm(1)
